    "md5": "d7f03ddc37d90c8e31dd7bfc933fad96",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1929,
    "chunk_text": "global success still eluding our performers\n\nNowadays, our artistes give the impression that projecting themselves on social media with music videos that primarily highlight their dancing ability is mandatory for their popularity.\n\nHowever, the question being asked is are they singers or dancers? Their new venture is not going to give them international success and, by international success, I mean making their presence felt where it matters most \u2013 the international charts \u2026 and global recognition.\n\nTake for instance K-pop \u2026 yes, our music scene has been around long before K-pop cropped up but K-pop is now streets ahead of us, dominating the international scene, and the charts, as well.\n\nK-pop\u2019s global success can be attributed to several key factors where their agencies have effectively utilised platforms, like YouTube, Twitter, and Instagram, to reach international audiences, releasing high-quality music videos, behind-the-scenes content, and interactive fan experiences.\n\nCollaborations with Western artistes, like Halsey, Dua Lipa, and Travis Scott, have also introduced K-pop to new audiences, blending K-pop\u2019s signature sounds with global pop, hip-hop, and EDM elements.\n\nWhat\u2019s more, K-pop\u2019s focus on polished production quality, intricate choreography, and visually stunning music videos, has set new standards for the music industry, influencing artistes and producers worldwide.\n\nK-pop groups regularly embark on world tours, performing in major cities across North America, Europe, and Latin America, solidifying their international fanbase.\n\nWith groups like BTS, BLACKPINK, and Stray Kids dominating international charts and breaking records, K-pop\u2019s influence on the global music industry is undeniable.\n\nAlston Koch, now residing in Australia, and Bill Forbes, who migrated to England, are both notable Sri Lankan singers who have made significant contributions to the music industry."
