    "chunk_size_bytes": 1320,
    "chunk_text": "The Minister of Health and Mass Media Nalinda Jayatissa\u2019s proposal to amend the Sri Lanka Press Council Act including provisions on the main issues of the requirement that the correction decided after inquiries of a newspaper report has also should be disseminated through electronic online media if they had carried the news published in newspapers.\n\nOver the years, successive governments have been accused of using the Press Council Act selectively against critical newspapers and journalists, especially during times of political tension or conflict.\n\nWhile advocates of the Act justify it as necessary to maintain responsibility in journalism and to curb misinformation, media rights groups both locally and internationally have consistently opposed it, viewing it as an outdated and authoritarian instrument.\n\nThe law applies exclusively to the print media, leaving broadcast and digital media outside its purview, which further raises questions about its relevance in today\u2019s diverse media landscape.\n\nDespite repeated calls for reform or repeal, the Act remains in place, symbolizing the uneasy relationship between the Sri Lankan state and the free press, and fuelling concerns that regulation is often used as a mechanism of control rather than a safeguard of ethical standards. (Colombo/September 09/2025)"
