    "md5": "53c599676878b76880c78738c81a4b9f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1990,
    "chunk_text": "Probe all crimes;recover all losses\n\nJustice Minister Harshana Nanayakkara has gone on record as saying that former political leaders ruined the country and, therefore, they must not be looked after at the expense of the public. He has sought to justify the newly passed law that strips former Presidents of their perks and privileges. Interestingly, the JVP aligned itself with the political parties of the former Presidents it is now inveighing against. Some of the JVP seniors were in the Cabinet of Chandrika Bandaranaike Kumaratunga\u2019s government from 2004 to 2005, and the JVP was instrumental in enabling Mahinda Rajapaksa to secure the executive presidency in 2005.\n\nIt facilitated the victory of Maithripala Sirisena in the 2015 presidential election, and was on very intimate terms with Prime Minister Ranil Wickremesinghe; it had representation in the National Executive Council of the UNP-led Yahapalana government, which was responsible for the Treasury bond scams. It had no qualms about throwing a lifeline to the UNP-led government, which President Sirisena tried to topple in 2018 after falling out with Prime Minister Wickremesinghe.\n\nThe JVP\u2019s reign of terror claimed thousands of lives and destroyed public assets worth billions of rupees in the late 1980s, but the state has looked after JVP leader Rohana Wijeweera\u2019s widow and her children since 1989. What have Minister Nanayakkara and JVP leaders got to say to this?\n\nPoliticians are notorious for exaggeration and overgeneralisation, and Minister Nanayakkara is no exception. But there is a school of thought that the political leaders who have ruled this country during the past several decades are responsible for the current mess, and therefore state funds should not be used to maintain them.\n\nNone of the former Presidents losing their perks and privileges will be driven to destitution. One can only hope that the government will not curtail security provided to them in a bid to settle political scores."
  },
