    "md5": "863b7a7ebab0f2db416a2e077972b0a6",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1850,
    "chunk_text": "None of the former Presidents losing their perks and privileges will be driven to destitution. One can only hope that the government will not curtail security provided to them in a bid to settle political scores.\n\nWe suggest that the assets of all politicians, especially the ex-Presidents/Prime Ministers, their progeny, and former state officials be probed, and losses they have caused to the country, if any, be recovered from them. The JVP has unearthed the Batalanda Commission report on torture chambers that existed in the late 1980s, and is demanding legal action against former President Ranil Wickremesinghe, on the basis of its findings.\n\nSo, the JVP\u2019s past crimes too must be probed. Most of all, huge losses the state suffered due to the JVP\u2019s terror campaign (1987-89) must be estimated and recovered. After all, President Anura Kumara Dissanayake, speaking at a ceremony to mark the 159th Police Day, on 03 September, declared that serious crimes were not subject to a statute of limitations and remained prosecutable indefinitely, and his government would ensure that no crime went unpunished.\n\nThe Opposition should pressure the NPP government to set up a special presidential commission of inquiry to probe the wanton destruction of public property, etc., in the late 1980s. Parliament is the best forum where this issue should be taken up. Unless the government cooperates, the Opposition should make the recovery of staggering losses caused by the southern terrorists a main plank of its election platform and seek a mandate from the people to achieve that end. No crime must go unpunished, as President Dissanayake has rightly said.\n\nLeave a Reply Cancel replyYour email address will not be published. Required fields are marked *Comment * Name *\n\nSave my name, email, and website in this browser for the next time I comment."
