    "md5": "75d04ff839f8c3b6e09f4bf1be5ea567",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1925,
    "chunk_text": "From marginalised voices to model UN champions: Sri Lankan youth break barriers at SLMUN 2025\n\nEighteen youth advocates from marginalized communities, supported by Child Action Lanka (CAL), transformed from local activists into award-winning diplomats at the 18th Sri Lanka Model United Nations (SLMUN) conference, held on August 16-17, 2025.\n\nTheir participation was sponsored by the SLMUN Executive Committee as a direct result of their powerful advocacy at Sri Lanka\u2019s first Child Rights Council, convened by CAL in April 2025.\n\nRepresenting CAL centers from Colombo, Killinochchi, Mullaitivu, Batticaloa, Nuwara Eliya, Kandy, and Bogawantalawa, the delegates overcame significant obstacles to prepare. With limited access to technology and research facilities, they relied on dedicated mentorship from CAL volunteers to master diplomatic protocol and complex global issues.\n\nThis journey was made possible through critical partnerships. Gateway College Colombo provided essential accommodation and venue support, while Hameedia outfitted all 18 delegates in formal business attire, ensuring they could participate with confidence and dignity.\n\nTheir preparation culminated in extraordinary success. Fourteen-year-old Shakila from CAL Colombo CDC won the prestigious Best Delegate Award in the UN Human Rights Council committee on her first attempt.\n\nSLMUN organizers noted the unique perspective the cohort brought, stating, \u201cTheir contributions felt more authentic and reflected lived experiences.\u201d\n\nOne delegate shared, \u201cWe experienced a world beyond our homes and associated with peers from Colombo. It was transformative.\u201d\n\nThis initiative proves that genuine inclusion requires investment in capacity building and supportive environments. These young advocates now return to their communities equipped with enhanced skills, confidence, and a broader awareness of their rights to champion grassroots change."
