    "chunk_text": "Bourse\u2019s bullish momentum sustained by conducive macroeconomic factors\n\nCSE activities were extremely bullish yesterday due to conducive macroeconomic conditions coupled with favourable GDP growth predicted in the second quarter of the year, according to market sources. Amid those developments both indices moved upwards. The All Share Price Index went up by 185.30 points while S and P SL20 rose by 70.88 points.\n\nTurnover stood at Rs 6.8 billion with eight crossings. Those crossings were reported in Chevron Lubricants where 1.7 million shares crossed to the tune of Rs 293 million; its shares traded at Rs 170, HNB (Non- Voting) 710,000 shares crossed to the tune of Rs 208.7 million and its shares traded at Rs 294, Sampath Bank 700 million shares crossed for Rs 98 million; its shares traded at Rs 140, Central Finance 321,000 shares crossed for Rs 90.1 million; its shares sold at Rs 280.\n\nEAP Finance 500,000 shares crossed to the tune of Rs 80.5 million; its shares traded at Rs 161, Lankem Developments two million shares crossed for Rs 55.5 million; its shares traded at Rs 28, Prime Lands Residencies one million shares crossed to the tune of Rs 34 million; its shares sold at Rs 34 and Singer (SriLanka) 271,000 shares crossed for Rs 22.3 million; its shares sold at Rs 82.50.\n\nIn the retail market top seven companies that mainly contributed to the turnover were; First Capital Treasury Rs 294 million (8.1 million shares traded), First Capital Holdings Rs 256 million (4.5 million shares traded), Lanka Credit and Business Finance Rs 241 million (39.7 million shares traded), Sierra Cables Rs 229 million (7.3 million shares traded), Commercial Lands Rs 227 million (1.2 million shares traded), Hemas Holdings Rs 174 million (5.4 million shares traded) and Asia Siyaka Rs 164 million (15 million shares traded). During the day 254 million shares volumes changed hands in 45000 transactions."
