    "chunk_text": "Curiously, most of the politicians with assets that ordinary people can only dream of acquiring are without any known sources of income. Some of them say they have inherited wealth, and their claims remind us of Daisy  Aachchi\u2019s bag of gems. How they have amassed so much wealth is a big question. Politics has obviously become El Dorado for many individuals across the political spectrum.\n\nAn attempt is being made in some quarters to use the assets of the JVP leaders to bolster the view that they no longer belong to the proletariat, and over the years they have become part of the political elite. The 2024 regime change in Sri Lanka has been described as a victory of the proletariat over the bourgeoisie or aristocracy. Even some religious dignitaries have sought to propagate this view. A better description of what we witnessed last year may be a victory of the emerging elite over the entrenched elite.\n\nWealth alone does not make elites. There is no universally accepted definition of elites, but the general consensus is that their most defining characteristics are disproportionate influence, control over resources, decision-making power, prestige and ability to reproduce their status across time. Some prominent non-JVP members of the NPP fit this description.\n\nThe JVP used the NPP as a vehicle to ascend to power. In 2004, the JVP pulled out an impressive electoral performance, with the help of the political elite represented by the then President Chandrika Bandaranaike Kumaratunga of aristocratic descent. Some JVP seniors are in the process of leaving their proletariat cocoon, as it were, and this political and ideological eclosion is evident from their wardrobe makeover and other lifestyle changes."
  },
