    "md5": "8e60e4c4d642fbf184a6213503992fb4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1504,
    "chunk_text": "The JVP used the NPP as a vehicle to ascend to power. In 2004, the JVP pulled out an impressive electoral performance, with the help of the political elite represented by the then President Chandrika Bandaranaike Kumaratunga of aristocratic descent. Some JVP seniors are in the process of leaving their proletariat cocoon, as it were, and this political and ideological eclosion is evident from their wardrobe makeover and other lifestyle changes.\n\nTime was when the leaders of Sri Lanka\u2019s main political parties came under this category; they have lost power to the \u2018Foxes\u2019, who are a cunning, innovative and manipulative lot, using persuasion, cleverness and new ideas to capture and consolidate power. According to Pareto, a society remains stable and prosperous so long as there is a healthy circulation of the aforesaid types of elites, but if they become closed, inept, corrupt and resistant to change, revolutions follow, with the new groups overthrowing them.\n\nPolitical parties also receive bribes in the form of campaign funds and various sponsorships for countless events, but only individual politicians are required to declare their assets. There is a pressing need to make it mandatory for all political parties to disclose their assets. One may recall that the UNP, during its long stay in the political wilderness, was reduced to penury, so much so that it could not even pay the utility bills at its headquarters, Sirikotha, towards the end of President Mahinda Rajapaksa\u2019s rule."
