    "chunk_size_bytes": 1461,
    "chunk_text": "Political parties also receive bribes in the form of campaign funds and various sponsorships for countless events, but only individual politicians are required to declare their assets. There is a pressing need to make it mandatory for all political parties to disclose their assets. One may recall that the UNP, during its long stay in the political wilderness, was reduced to penury, so much so that it could not even pay the utility bills at its headquarters, Sirikotha, towards the end of President Mahinda Rajapaksa\u2019s rule.\n\nPolitical parties in power are known to maintain huge cash reserves, and this is why they do not resort to demonetising high-value currency notes, known as the purge of big bills, to flush out black money. Most of the newly issued 2,000-rupee notes are also likely to end up in the cash reserves of political parties, their members and other wealthy individuals.\n\nWhat the asset declarations at issue signify is that politicians of all hues have the wherewithal to look after themselves and their families. The ordinary public should stop deifying politicians and making sacrifices for political parties of their choice at the expense of the wellbeing of their loved ones, work harder, earn more, live comfortably and build up nest eggs.\n\nLeave a Reply Cancel replyYour email address will not be published. Required fields are marked *Comment * Name *\n\nSave my name, email, and website in this browser for the next time I comment."
