    "chunk_size_bytes": 1853,
    "chunk_text": "Assets and duplicity\n\nThere is no dearth of issues in this country. However, they come with a short shelf life; they usually last for about two weeks. They either fizzle out or are eclipsed by new ones. A narcotic issue that JVP/NPP flogged hard in a bid to discredit the SLPP and its leaders has given way to a media feeding frenzy over some government politicians\u2019 assets. The Opposition MPs, except a few, have also submitted their asset declarations, but the media remains intensely focused only on those of the ruling party members. Politicians who wield power tend to receive harsher press than their opponents. That is the way the cookie crumbles.\n\nThe JVP-led NPP government, which ruthlessly mixed jabs, hooks and uppercuts to overwhelm the Opposition on the propaganda front, is now ducking desperately, with the latter throwing a flurry of punches aggressively, some of them below the belt. The critics of the government are asking how the JVP/NPP leaders, who have acquired substantial amounts of wealth, can claim that the post-Independence period has been a curse for them. The JVP, which had the public believe that their leaders\u2019 lot was no better than that of the masses, finds itself in an embarrassing position, with its propagandists struggling to launch a counterattack against the Opposition. Perhaps, they need not worry or exert themselves; another mega controversy is bound to come about sooner than expected overshadowing the asset issue.\n\nThe JVP/NPP has sought to make a virtue of necessity by claiming that the Opposition has got hold of something to beat its leaders with because they declared their assets in keeping with their commitment to upholding transparency. They have disclosed their assets to the Commission to Investigate Allegations of Bribery or Corruption because the elections laws require them to do so."
  },
