    "md5": "39ea5d7d4010135ae2a0b022df1c0287",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1615,
    "chunk_text": "The JVP/NPP has sought to make a virtue of necessity by claiming that the Opposition has got hold of something to beat its leaders with because they declared their assets in keeping with their commitment to upholding transparency. They have disclosed their assets to the Commission to Investigate Allegations of Bribery or Corruption because the elections laws require them to do so.\n\nSome Opposition politicians are demanding to know how Minister Wasantha Samarasinghe, who once claimed in a television interview, that he had sold wade in trains in Anuradhapura during the Poson season, has acquired so much wealth; Samarasinghe\u2019s explanation is that he has inherited land and shops in Thambuttegama. A former minister among the Opposition MPs has also said he started life as a wade vendor in trains in Badulla before taking to politics. He is a wealthy person today, and the question is how he and his family members have enriched themselves. They, too, should be probed. Another former Minister, currently in the Opposition, worked as a driver for a deputy minister in the Chandrika Kumaratunga government; he entered politics and went on to become a Cabinet Minister. He has more assets than Minister Samarasinghe. He is also bashing the JVP/NPP.\n\nSri Lankan politicians seem to think there is a sucker born every minute, and therefore they can go on duping the public indefinitely. But they cannot fool all the people all of the time. Let the holier-than-thou worthies in the Opposition be urged to turn the searchlight inwards. They are without any moral right to be critical of others\u2019 ill-gotten gains."
  },
