    "chunk_size_bytes": 1985,
    "chunk_text": "Poland\u2013Sri Lanka Relations: Beyond Tea and Tourism?\n\nOxford, United Kingdom\u2013 While preparing for my next trip to Sri Lanka, I keep reflecting on relations between Poland and the \u201cPearl of the Indian Ocean.\u201d At first, Sri Lanka and my home country\u2014a democratic nation of 38 million people in Central Europe\u2014may seem very distant from one another. Yet, this year has brought a substantial development in their bilateral relations.\n\nIn May 2025, Polish Foreign Minister Radoslaw Sikorski visited Sri Lanka. He participated in high-level meetings with President Anura Kumara Dissanayake, Prime Minister Harini Amarasuriya, and Foreign Minister Vijitha Herath. Notably, he was representing not only Warsaw but also the entire European Union, as Poland held the EU presidency.\n\nShared historical connections between Sri Lanka and Poland stretch back more than a century. One of them is particularly close to my heart: the visit of Adam Asnyk, a renowned poet born in my hometown of Kalisz, the oldest city in Poland. He travelled to Sri Lanka in 1894.\n\nEven more notable was the 1914 journey of Bronislaw Malinowski, the world-famous anthropologist and founder of the functionalist school. During his stay on the way to the western Pacific, he visited Anuradhapura, Dambulla, Kandy, Sigiriya, and even the ancient village of Nalanda in Matale.\n\nMalinowski was joined by the brilliant but tormented artist Stanislaw Witkiewicz, popularly known as Witkacy. Devastated by the suicide of his fianc\u00e9e, Witkacy contemplated ending his own life in Ceylon. However, he did not follow through. We will never know whether it was the island\u2019s beauty that restrained him from such a tragic act. But Sri Lanka undeniably left its mark on his work\u2014inspiring a series of paintings and even a novel. Apparently, it was Witkacy\u2019s destiny to end his life by suicide after all. Upon hearing of the Soviet invasion of Poland on September 17, 1939, Witkacy committed suicide the following day."
