    "chunk_text": "Malinowski was joined by the brilliant but tormented artist Stanislaw Witkiewicz, popularly known as Witkacy. Devastated by the suicide of his fianc\u00e9e, Witkacy contemplated ending his own life in Ceylon. However, he did not follow through. We will never know whether it was the island\u2019s beauty that restrained him from such a tragic act. But Sri Lanka undeniably left its mark on his work\u2014inspiring a series of paintings and even a novel. Apparently, it was Witkacy\u2019s destiny to end his life by suicide after all. Upon hearing of the Soviet invasion of Poland on September 17, 1939, Witkacy committed suicide the following day.\n\nAt present, Polish people are increasingly perceiving Sri Lanka as an attractive tourist destination. For a short period of time prior to the Covid-19 pandemic, the LOT Polish Airlines operated direct flights between Warsaw and Colombo. Unfortunately, currently there are no direct flights. However, connections with a single stopover in the Middle East remain widely available. This surely helped Poland become the tenth-largest source of tourists to Sri Lanka in 2024.\n\nIn fact, Sri Lanka has always been part of Polish homes. It is connected to one of the most important habits: drinking tea. Poland is among Europe\u2019s most tea-loving nations. Interestingly, we neither call it \u201cchai\u201d nor \u201ctea,\u201d but \u201cherbata.\u201d This word is derived from \u201cherbs.\u201d Tea is a must-have beverage in Polish households\u2014with Ceylon tea regarded as the best in quality.\n\nThe ties between the two democracies are still evolving. As a member-state of the European Union, Poland is increasingly attractive to Sri Lankan professionals. As of the end of 2024, approximately 1,600 Sri Lankans worked in Poland. These statistics may not be impressive; however, over the last decade the number of Sri Lankan workers in Poland has increased nearly 30-fold."
