    "md5": "edfce56496bc9ef57a38c1800f47d3f4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1890,
    "chunk_text": "India-Sri Lanka Foundation approves several new projects, including in fisheries sector\n\nECONOMYNEXT \u2013 The India\u2013Sri Lanka Foundation (ISLF) has approved several new proposals received by the Colombo and New Delhi Secretariats at its 40th meeting held in Colombo recently.\n\n\u201cThe Board reviewed the progress of ongoing projects and approved several new proposals received by the Colombo and New Delhi Secretariats across diverse sectors including culture, education, health, environment, agriculture, and capacity building,\u201d the High Commission of India, Colombo said in a statement.\n\nThe session was co-chaired by the High Commissioner of India to Sri Lanka Santosh Jha, and the High Commissioner of Sri Lanka to India Mahishini Colonne.\n\n\u201cNotable approved initiatives encompass academic collaborations in library science and visual and performing arts, cultural workshops and tourism promotion activities in both countries, microbiome engineering research to prevent plant diseases in Sri Lanka, cancer and menstrual health awareness programs for vulnerable communities, training opportunities for Sri Lankan health professionals in India, women\u2019s empowerment workshops and support\n\nprogrammes for underprivileged communities, capacity building initiatives for fisheries officials to strengthen the sector, and knowledge sharing\n\nprograms in science and environmental fields alongside mangrove restoration projects in coastal regions.\u201d\n\nISLF was established in December 1998 by an agreement signed between the Governments of India and Sri Lanka to foster bilateral relations through economic, scientific, educational, technical and cultural cooperation, and the promotion of people-to-people ties between the two countries.\n\nThe foundation has extended support for around 600 projects worth over 200 million rupees, with more than 360 projects being implemented in Sri Lanka."
  },
