    "md5": "6d02151fe3482d3aa5df97d6ead1486a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1839,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka is not doing open market operations yet, though macro-economists have got themselves the legal power to do so by midnight gazette, under Sri Lanka\u2019s controversial new monetary law backed by the International Monetary Fund.\n\nCountries with strong currencies and run deflationary policy in some cases, generally have very low interest rates.\n\nIf rates are allowed to go up from time to time, and monetary stability is maintained, Sri Lanka\u2019s interest rates could go back to pre-central bank levels or at least close to that given the inflationism in the US from abundant reserves.\n\nSri Lanka also had low interest comparable to developed nations when the central bank was created and 20-year bonds were sold at 3 percent and short-term ones at 1.5 percent.\n\nThis was the age of innocence before macro-economists had developed the concept of permanent inflation and \u2018real\u2019 interest rates which required not just higher nominal rates but also floating rates.\n\nIt was also before the vicious concept of \u2018core inflation\u2019 was developed with wildly volatile commodity prices after the collapse of the Smithsonian in particular.\n\nSri Lanka consumer price index was mostly food at the time and (except during the Liberty bonds bubble when rice prices suddenly shot up along with tea), food prices were peaceful.\n\nIn 2025, bond sales are going out of the hands of the central bank to the Treasury, however since the debt office officials are trained by the central bank, questionable practices such as the buffer strategy or the current problem with bills could continue to take place.\n\nHowever at least the government is raining money through bonds, though at higher rates.\n\nWhat macro-economists have achieved in pushing up rates and destroying the currency with \u2018rate cuts\u2019 and similar action is remarkable, analysts say."
